WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.260 --> 00:00:05.220
Tim Allen's success on the standup circuit
led to him starring in the long-running sitcom

00:00:05.220 --> 00:00:08.020
Home Improvement, which gave him nationwide
fame.

00:00:08.020 --> 00:00:12.280
He eventually went on to star as the voice
of Buzz Lightyear in Disney's beloved Toy

00:00:12.280 --> 00:00:16.059
Story movies and as Santa in the Santa Clause
franchise.

00:00:16.059 --> 00:00:20.280
His comic talent and everyman charm made it
seem like he was poised to become a major

00:00:20.280 --> 00:00:21.350
movie star.

00:00:21.350 --> 00:00:25.050
But over the past decade, he's almost disappeared
from the big screen.

00:00:25.050 --> 00:00:30.050
Here's a look at why you won't see too many
movies starring Tim Allen anymore.

00:00:30.050 --> 00:00:31.720
His shtick is old

00:00:31.720 --> 00:00:36.679
Tim Allen has always been identified with
a very specific type of character: the ordinary,

00:00:36.679 --> 00:00:39.190
politically incorrect man's man.

00:00:39.190 --> 00:00:43.769
That made him a big hit in the '80s and '90s
on television, but hasn't translated to modern

00:00:43.769 --> 00:00:48.920
movie audiences, which according to the MPAA'S
research, are attended by more women than

00:00:48.920 --> 00:00:49.920
men.

00:00:49.920 --> 00:00:51.530
Which doesn't quite suit Allen's style.

00:00:51.530 --> 00:00:57.940
"The bathroom has everything, including the
brand new Lazy Bowl reclining toilet."

00:00:57.940 --> 00:01:04.890
So it's not really a surprise that Allen has
stuck with television instead, where he can

00:01:04.890 --> 00:01:08.780
still safely target that same male demographic.

00:01:08.780 --> 00:01:10.470
Third in line

00:01:10.470 --> 00:01:14.890
Even when Allen was at his absolute hottest,
he was rarely anyone's first choice for film

00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:15.890
roles.

00:01:15.890 --> 00:01:19.850
For instance, The Santa Clause was reportedly
written with Bill Murray in mind.

00:01:19.850 --> 00:01:24.100
When he passed, a long-standing rumor says
it was offered to Chevy Chase before finally

00:01:24.100 --> 00:01:25.390
going to Allen.

00:01:25.390 --> 00:01:29.720
And he allegedly was third in line for Buzz
Lightyear too, with Pixar pitching the role

00:01:29.720 --> 00:01:32.840
to both Jim Carrey and Billy Crystal first.

00:01:32.840 --> 00:01:36.500
Considering how difficult it was for him to
get good roles even when he was at the height

00:01:36.500 --> 00:01:41.770
of his popularity, it's no wonder he seems
to have even fewer opportunities now.

00:01:41.770 --> 00:01:43.990
Big screen disappointment

00:01:43.990 --> 00:01:48.720
While the Toy Story and Santa Clause franchises
have both been big hits, most of Allen's other

00:01:48.720 --> 00:01:53.830
movies have been either critical or box office
disappointments — and usually both.

00:01:53.830 --> 00:01:58.830
His most recent efforts, like Crazy on the
Outside and 3 Geezers!, had brief or nonexistent

00:01:58.830 --> 00:02:04.760
theatrical runs, and his filmography is littered
with movies like Jungle 2 Jungle, Wild Hogs,

00:02:04.760 --> 00:02:10.530
and Christmas With the Kranks—all of which
currently sit below 20 percent on Rotten Tomatoes.

00:02:10.530 --> 00:02:14.310
Even when critics like his films, they don't
always hit it big with fans.

00:02:14.310 --> 00:02:20.120
The martial arts drama Redbelt, for instance,
grossed less than $2.5 million despite favorable

00:02:20.120 --> 00:02:21.120
reviews.

00:02:21.120 --> 00:02:25.760
And while Galaxy Quest has become a true cult
classic, with many science fiction fans counting

00:02:25.760 --> 00:02:31.430
it as one of their favorites, it only made
a modest $71 million against a $45 million

00:02:31.430 --> 00:02:35.659
budget — respectable, but hardly enough
to change Allen's reputation as someone whose

00:02:35.659 --> 00:02:40.120
success comes from his franchises instead
of the other way around.

00:02:40.120 --> 00:02:41.459
Played out

00:02:41.459 --> 00:02:45.769
Both of Allen's big franchises seem to be
pretty much played out, artistically if not

00:02:45.769 --> 00:02:46.900
financially.

00:02:46.900 --> 00:02:51.209
Every installment of the Santa Clause trilogy
got worse reviews and made less money than

00:02:51.209 --> 00:02:52.400
the previous one.

00:02:52.400 --> 00:02:56.879
And while Toy Story 4 is in production, it
seems kind of like a cash grab considering

00:02:56.879 --> 00:03:00.519
Toy Story 3 clearly was intended as a series
coda.

00:03:00.519 --> 00:03:04.580
Even if it becomes the massive box office
hit everyone expects, that probably won't

00:03:04.580 --> 00:03:09.209
translate to new film roles for Allen — though
it will mean he gets to milk the series for

00:03:09.209 --> 00:03:15.319
even more voicework in Toy Story shorts, made-for-TV
movies, video games, and rides at Disney World.

00:03:15.319 --> 00:03:17.640
Hey, money is money.

00:03:17.640 --> 00:03:19.549
Family friendly flops

00:03:19.549 --> 00:03:24.379
Allen primarily achieved fame through family-friendly
comedy on Home Improvement, and he helped

00:03:24.379 --> 00:03:28.200
cement "family friendly" as his big-screen
genre with Toy Story.

00:03:28.200 --> 00:03:31.349
However, a number of other all-ages projects
fizzled.

00:03:31.349 --> 00:03:37.520
In 2006, for example, Allen appeared in three
movies: The Shaggy Dog, Santa Clause 3, and

00:03:37.520 --> 00:03:38.520
Zoom.

00:03:38.520 --> 00:03:42.390
While The Shaggy Dog and Santa Clause 3 at
least earned their budgets back at the box

00:03:42.390 --> 00:03:47.269
office, Zoom was a complete disaster, and
all three were critically panned, to the point

00:03:47.269 --> 00:03:52.340
where Allen earned a Worst Actor nomination
at the Razzies for his collective incompetence.

00:03:52.340 --> 00:03:54.170
That's… pretty unfriendly.

00:03:54.170 --> 00:03:57.060
"You are a sad, strange little man.

00:03:57.120 --> 00:03:58.420
And you have my pity.

00:03:58.640 --> 00:03:59.640
Farewell!"

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:02.720
Works well with others

00:04:02.730 --> 00:04:05.699
Allen works best as part of an ensemble cast.

00:04:05.699 --> 00:04:09.120
For example, his portrayal of Buzz Lightyear
wouldn't be half as charming without having

00:04:09.120 --> 00:04:13.689
Tom Hanks' Woody to bounce off of or without
his interaction with the rest of the colorful

00:04:13.689 --> 00:04:14.689
cast.

00:04:14.689 --> 00:04:18.540
His critically beloved turn in Galaxy Quest
was so successful in large part because he

00:04:18.540 --> 00:04:23.380
was playing off of some very heavy hitters,
including Sigourney Weaver and Alan Rickman.

00:04:23.380 --> 00:04:24.380
"C'mon!

00:04:24.380 --> 00:04:27.200
Without my crew, I'm not a commander, eh?"

00:04:27.200 --> 00:04:32.320
Even Wild Hogs, a critical pariah, was financially
successful primarily because it also featured

00:04:32.320 --> 00:04:35.780
Martin Lawrence, John Travolta, and William
H. Macy.

00:04:35.780 --> 00:04:40.420
If you were creating a recipe for a hit movie,
Allen is less of a key ingredient and more

00:04:40.420 --> 00:04:42.570
of a condiment or garnish.

00:04:42.570 --> 00:04:43.780
Busy TV career

00:04:43.780 --> 00:04:48.780
Of course, perhaps the most obvious reason
that Tim Allen isn't regularly starring in

00:04:48.780 --> 00:04:51.520
movies is that he, well, just doesn't have
to.

00:04:51.520 --> 00:04:55.780
In 2010, he tried his hand at directing with
Crazy on the Outside.

00:04:55.780 --> 00:05:01.050
When that flopped, he simply returned to TV
in 2011 with Last Man Standing, a sort of

00:05:01.050 --> 00:05:05.910
spiritual reboot of Home Improvement where
Allen brings his blue collar alpha male schtick

00:05:05.910 --> 00:05:07.040
into the 21st century.

00:05:07.040 --> 00:05:08.750
"He's a man's man.

00:05:08.750 --> 00:05:10.430
But lately, he's realizing…"

00:05:10.430 --> 00:05:12.350
"What happened to men?"

00:05:12.350 --> 00:05:14.840
"... he's not in a man's world anymore."

00:05:14.840 --> 00:05:19.200
The show, which Allen executive produces as
well as stars in, has been critically panned

00:05:19.200 --> 00:05:20.360
from the beginning.

00:05:20.360 --> 00:05:25.060
And yet, it shows absolutely no signs of slowing
down, with six seasons as of the making of

00:05:25.060 --> 00:05:26.060
this video.

00:05:26.060 --> 00:05:30.600
With Last Man Standing offering steady work
for steady ratings, we're guessing Allen isn't

00:05:30.600 --> 00:05:34.460
eager to run away from his small-screen success
anytime soon.

00:05:34.460 --> 00:05:35.850
